d injection vulnerabilities by supplying malicious input to operating system commands executed by an application. Successful exploitation can allow attackers to retrieve sensitive data, escalate privileges, and move laterally within a network. Strong input validation is the preferred defense against command injection vulnerabilities. Avoiding operating system command execution entirely is the most effective mitigation. What is OS Command Injection? OS Command Injection is a web application vulnerability that allows attackers to execute arbitrary commands on the underlying operating system. Because successful exploitation can lead to full command execution on a target system, command injection vulnerabilities are often considered among the most severe web application vulnerabilities. For a concise definition, see Fastly's Learning Center entry on OS Command Injection. This blog goes deeper: below, we'll walk through real-world CVE case studies, the mechanics of an exploitation, and what these attacks look like when observed by a WAF in production traffic. This is a common pattern in web applications that perform operating system command execution on behalf of users, and it can become dangerous when user input is passed directly to shell commands. Consider a web application meant to monitor internal systems and provide alerts when one of the systems goes offline. In this scenario, the application may want to test network reachability to the",
